1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,000
We start with Qwen zero point eight B. This first card frames the model as
a Transformer trace: text becomes tokens, tokens move through stacked
blocks, and the output is a probability distribution for the next token.

2
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The same prompt stays at the top of the screen, so every view is answering
one question: given this context, what should the next token be? The
tutorial card follows that generation process step by step.

3
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:58,000
The embedding pages show the first conversion. Each word piece is mapped
into a learned vector. In the diagram, those vectors are the small
rectangles feeding the block, one row for each token in the prompt.

4
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The Transformer block card is important for Qwen. Qwen zero point eight B
has stacked blocks, but not every layer is full attention. In this trace,
full attention appears only at selected layers, which is why the attention
view may label the nearest full-attention block.

5
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The attention section is the classic Transformer mechanism. Each token
builds query, key, and value streams. Queries ask what the current token
needs, keys describe what previous tokens offer, and values carry the
content that will be mixed.

6
00:01:48,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The Q K V card expands that calculation. The same token embeddings are
multiplied by learned weights to produce queries, keys, and values. The
visual goal is to make the matrix multiplication visible, not just name
it.

7
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The multi-head card shows why the model splits attention into heads. Each
head can focus on a different relation in the prompt, such as position,
phrase structure, or the likely continuation after the final word.

8
00:02:44,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Masked self-attention turns token-to-token comparisons into a causal
table. Future tokens are blocked, previous tokens are weighted, and the
visible dot pattern is the model's current view of which earlier tokens
matter.

9
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:36,000
After attention, the MLP refines each token independently. The card notes
that Qwen uses a gated MLP with a SiLU-style activation, so this stage is
not another attention table; it is a channel-wise transformation.

10
00:03:36,000 --> 00:04:04,000
The output pages move from hidden vectors to logits and then
probabilities. Logits are raw scores over the vocabulary. Softmax converts
them into a distribution, and the bars show which next tokens are most
likely.

11
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The probability and sampling cards explain the final choice. Temperature
reshapes the distribution: lower temperature concentrates probability on
the top candidates, while higher temperature gives weaker candidates more
room.

12
00:04:38,000 --> 00:05:18,000
The remaining Qwen cards cover sampling, residual paths, normalization,
and dropout. These are support systems: residuals keep information
flowing, normalization keeps scales controlled, and dropout is a training-
time regularizer, not an inference-time operation.

13
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:50,000
This completes the Transformer side of the comparison. The key picture is
token-to-token interaction: Qwen compares tokens through attention,
refines channels through the MLP, and then scores the next token.

14
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Now the recording switches to RWKV seven, the zero point one B comparison
model. The card makes the central difference explicit: RWKV is recurrent.
It processes tokens one at a time and carries a compact state forward.

15
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:45,000
The RWKV architecture card reorganizes the same language-model task.
Embeddings still start the process and probabilities still finish it, but
the middle is an RWKV block with Time Mix, recurrent state, and Channel
Mix.

16
00:06:45,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Time Mix creates streams such as read, decay, erase, write, and gate.
These are not fake Q, K, and V matrices. They are the signals that decide
how the recurrent state should change at the next token.

17
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:32,000
The recurrent state card is the heart of the RWKV view. The state matrix
is decayed, an old component can be erased, and a new write is added. The
matrix view shows those terms; the manifold view projects how the state
moves across tokens.

18
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Channel Mix is the feed-forward side of the RWKV block. It mixes the
current hidden vector with the previous channel state, applies a squared
ReLU-style activation, and projects back to the residual stream.

19
00:07:48,000 --> 00:08:04,900
The final RWKV cards return to probabilities, sampling, residuals, and
normalization. The comparison is now clear: Qwen's Transformer view is
token-to-token attention, while RWKV's view is state transition over time.
